ation is fundamental to the process\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Recrystallization behavior of austenite is a fundamental aspect of controlled rolling. The process's stages depend on deformation in the recrystallization region, non-recrystallization region above Ar3, and the two-phase region, so understanding austenite recrystallization is essential to controlling microstructure [Data: Entities (0, 40); Relationships (38)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Anisotropy and embrittlement are notable risks\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Controlled rolling also produces dimensional anisotropy of the microstructure and is a main cause of through-thickness embrittlement. It can lead to a mixed structure in controlled-rolled steel, and in some steels α nucleation occurs at grain interiors as well as at grain boundaries. These effects are important for assessing the trade-offs of controlled rolling versus conventional processing [Data: Entities (0); Relationships (39)].\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}, "metrics": {"attempted_request_count": 1, "successful_response_count": 0, "failed_response_count": 0, "responses_with_tokens": 1, "prompt_tokens": 4295, "completion_tokens": 5876, "total_tokens": 10171, "compute_duration_seconds": 25.834497451782227, "streaming_responses": 0, "retries": 0, "requests_with_retries": 0}}}